# pennywise

Read a plain-text household ledger and see where the money went. Python 3.9+, standard library only.

    pip install .

gives you the `pennywise` library and command.

## Ledger format

    # household, March
    2024-03-01 salary 2500.00 March pay
    2024-03-02 groceries -42.50 weekly shop
    2024-03-05 Rent -900

`DATE CATEGORY AMOUNT [NOTE]`, separated by spaces or tabs. Amounts have at most two decimals
(`12`, `+12`, `-0.99`); positive is money in. Categories are case-insensitive and shown in lower
case. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored.

## Library

```python
import pennywise

entries = pennywise.parse(open("house.txt").read())   # raises pennywise.ParseError(.line)
pennywise.balance(entries)                             # Decimal
pennywise.by_category(entries); pennywise.by_month(entries)
pennywise.filter_entries(entries, category="rent", since=None, until=None)
```

## Command

    pennywise balance FILE [--json]
    pennywise summary FILE [--by category|month] [--json]
    pennywise list FILE [--category C] [--since DATE] [--until DATE] [--json]
    pennywise add FILE DATE CATEGORY AMOUNT [NOTE]
    pennywise export FILE [--format csv]

`FILE` may be `-` (standard input) except for `add`. Exit status: 0 success; 1 mistake in the
ledger or the entry (message on stderr names the line, nothing on stdout); 2 unreadable file or
bad command line.

